Till now what we did, creating one html form, and manually declare, and doing all the things. we added the method as 'GET/POSt', then passing a function as onSubmit  = {Function name which needs to be execute}, and manually passing the event, by doing the fooing things.
  
export function HtmlForm(){

    function handleForm(event){
        event.preventDefault()
        // getting control on current target 
        const formEl = event.currentTarget
        // getting the data from the From 
        const formData = new FormData(formEl)
        // to get a nice object of key value pair 
        // to check what are the values we are getting 
        // from a form 
        console.log(Object.fromEntries(formData))
        const id = formData.get("userId")
        const pass = formData.get("password")
        formEl.reset()
    }

    return (
        <div>
    <h3>Sign in Form</h3>
    <form onSubmit={handleForm} method="GET">
        <label htmlFor="userId">User Name</label>
        <input id="userId" type="text" name="userId"/>
        <label htmlFor="password">Password</label>
        <input id="password" type="password" name="password" />
        <button>Submit</button>
    </form>
    </div>
    )
}

But some of the things, which are very obvious like, if I am creating a form, I need the form data, then if it is a React application, I have to prevent default re-loeading of the browser, after getting the form data, I need to clear those input filed. All of these work we are doing manually. Which in explaind in the above code.
But after React 19 update, we have the option to pass a function as action of the form. Means what need to performed, if the form is submitted.

  export function HtmlForm(){

    function handleForm(formData){
        const id = formData.get("userId")
        const pass = formData.get("password")
        console.log(id, pass)
        // also we can include the backend steps, like sending emails, that 'You have signed up successfully'
        // to print all the data getting from the Form
        Object.fromEntries(formData)
    }

    return (
        <div>
    <h3>Sign in Form</h3>
    <form action={handleForm}>
        <label htmlFor="userId">User Name</label>
        <input id="userId" type="text" name="userId"/>
        <label htmlFor="password">Password</label>
        <input id="password" type="password" name="password" />
        <button>Submit</button>
    </form>
    </div>
    )
}
